+<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} breadcrumbName="Auto Glass Local SEO" extraSchema={extraSchema}>
+
+  <PageHero
+    id="local-seo"
+    eyebrow="Google Maps & Local Search"
+    heading="Auto Glass Local SEO That Generates More Nearby Opportunities"
+    lead="Drivers with urgent glass needs compare nearby businesses across Google Maps, reviews, service details, and websites before they call. We help your shop show up in the Map Pack for the locations and service areas you genuinely serve — and give drivers the reasons to choose you."
+    ctaLabel="See My Local Search Gaps"
+    supportLine="Complimentary Search + Lead Generation Opportunity Analysis"
+  />
+
+  <CapabilityGrid id="local-capabilities" heading="What auto glass local SEO covers" eyebrow="Core capabilities" cards={capabilities} />
+
+  <ProseSection id="why-maps" eyebrow="Why Maps matters" heading="For urgent auto glass searches, Google Maps is often the whole decision">
+    <p>
+      When a driver searches "windshield replacement near me" on a phone, Google usually shows three Maps results before anything else. Many drivers call from that list without ever visiting a website. That makes your Google Business Profile — its categories, services, hours, photos, reviews, and how close you appear to the driver — one of the highest-leverage assets in <a href="/auto-glass-lead-generation/">auto glass lead generation</a>.
+    </p>
+    <p>
+      Local SEO is the work of earning that placement in the markets you serve and making the listing convincing enough to win the call. It sits alongside <a href="/auto-glass-seo/">auto glass SEO</a> for the website and <a href="/google-ads/">Google Ads</a> for immediate demand, and all three depend on the same accurate business information.
+    </p>
+  </ProseSection>
+
+  <ProseSection id="gbp" eyebrow="Google Business Profile" heading="The Google Business Profile, done properly" alt>
+    <p>
+      Most of what drivers see in Maps comes from your profile. The details that matter for an auto glass business:
+    </p>
+    <ul class="prose-grid">
+      <li><strong>Primary and secondary categories</strong> The right auto glass categories so Google matches you to glass searches rather than general repair.</li>
+      <li><strong>Services</strong> Windshield replacement, repair, mobile service, side and back glass, and ADAS calibration listed explicitly so they appear in relevant searches.</li>
+      <li><strong>Business information</strong> Name, phone, address or service area, and hours that exactly match your website and other listings.</li>
+      <li><strong>Photos</strong> Real photos of the shop, mobile units, calibration equipment, and completed work — not stock images.</li>
+      <li><strong>Reviews and responses</strong> Recent, specific reviews from real customers, with responses that show a human is paying attention.</li>
+      <li><strong>Website link and landing page</strong> A link to the page that matches the location and services, with tap-to-call visible immediately.</li>
+    </ul>
+  </ProseSection>
+
+  <ProseSection id="proximity" eyebrow="Location, radius, and routing" heading="Proximity, service areas, and how your operation actually works">
+    <p>
+      Maps visibility is tied to where the driver is searching from, where your business is located, and the service areas you declare. How you set this up should mirror how you operate:
+    </p>
+    <ul>
+      <li><strong>Physical shop locations</strong> — each shop gets its own verified profile and location page; proximity to the searcher strongly influences which shop appears.</li>
+      <li><strong>Mobile-only or mobile-heavy businesses</strong> — a service-area profile with defined coverage areas, plus service-area pages on the website that describe those markets with real detail.</li>
+      <li><strong>Multi-location operations</strong> — consistent information across every listing, with each profile linked to the right location page so Google and drivers know which shop serves which market.</li>
+      <li><strong>Mobile routing and ADAS capability</strong> — if calibration is done at a specific shop, or mobile service covers a wider radius than the shop draws, the profile, service list, and website should say so; drivers and insurers searching for calibration need to know where it happens.</li>
+    </ul>
+    <p>
+      The goal is not to appear everywhere. It is to appear for the searches you can actually serve, and to avoid the thin, repetitive location pages that Google tends to discount.
+    </p>
+  </ProseSection>
+
+  <ProseSection id="local-authority" eyebrow="Local authority" heading="Reviews, citations, and consistency" alt>
+    <p>
+      Beyond the profile, Google looks for signals that your business is real, established, and consistent. Reviews are the most visible: recent ones that mention specific services help both ranking and conversion, and an ethical request process for real customers keeps them coming. Citations — your business information on relevant directories and industry sites — matter where they are accurate; a mismatched phone number or old address on a third-party site can undermine your listing. Location consistency across your website, profile, and citations is the quiet foundation of all of it.
+    </p>
+  </ProseSection>
+
+  <ProseSection id="local-measurement" eyebrow="Measurement" heading="Tracking local calls and inquiries">
+    <p>
+      Google Business Profile reports calls, direction requests, and website clicks, but those numbers alone do not tell you which were qualified. We pair profile data with call tracking on the website and, where appropriate, on the listing itself, so local calls can be reviewed for quality and attributed by location. Combined with organic and paid tracking, that gives a clear view of what Maps is producing in qualified leads and, where your shop shares booking data, booked jobs.
+    </p>
+  </ProseSection>
+
+  <RelatedLinks
