+      <li><strong>SEO architecture</strong> A structure search engines can crawl and understand, with one clear page for each thing drivers search for.</li>
+      <li><strong>Service architecture</strong> Windshield replacement, repair, rock chip, mobile service, side and back glass, ADAS calibration, insurance — each with its own substantive page.</li>
+      <li><strong>Location architecture</strong> Location pages for physical shops, service-area pages for mobile coverage, and internal links that tell Google which location serves which market.</li>
+      <li><strong>Technical SEO and speed</strong> Fast, mobile-first pages, clean headings and metadata, indexable URLs, no template bloat.</li>
+      <li><strong>Mobile UX</strong> Designed for a driver in a parking lot: tap-to-call above the fold, hours and service area obvious, forms short.</li>
+      <li><strong>Conversion pathways</strong> Calls and quote requests as first-class paths, with the insurance and ADAS questions that stall decisions answered on the page.</li>
+      <li><strong>Analytics</strong> Call tracking, form tracking and analytics events on every conversion so you can see which channels and pages produce qualified opportunities.</li>
+      <li><strong>Schema and AI/search accessibility</strong> Organization, service, location and FAQ structured data so search engines and AI-assisted discovery can read what you do and where.</li>
+    </ul>
+  </ProseSection>
+
